luding null; I rejected and closed H202. H203 is borderline: R194 is +0.0270 [-0.0039, 0.0579], just near the benchmark but still not interval-supported above null or above the benchmark. I left H203 unresolved and deferred, not as evidence for a clinically meaningful feature_045 effect.\n\nH204-H206 tested feature_045 on outcome_001, outcome_008, and outcome_011. R195 through R197 show estimates opposite or close to null with intervals that do not support the hypothesized stronger-dependency direction at clinically meaningful magnitudes. These do not support broadening feature_045 beyond a narrow, still-unresolved outcome_013 signal. I rejected and closed all three.\n\nFor new predictor screens, H207 and H209 test feature_006 and feature_029 on outcome_013. R198 and R200 provide no clinically meaningful interval-supported support for stronger outcome_013 dependency; both point estimates are small and intervals include null. I rejected and closed H207 and H209.\n\nH208 tests feature_006 on outcome_014. R199 gives signed estimate -0.0647 [-0.1186, -0.0108], which is interval-supported but opposite to the registered direction. It suggests feature_006=1 may be associated with stronger outcome_014 dependency, with absolute magnitude far above the outcome_014 benchmark. I rejected the original claim because it does not support the registered weaker-dependency hypothesis. I deferred rather than closed because it is a potentially important exploratory signal. Caution is required: outcome_014 has already yielded multiple unusually large or unexpected signals, including the implausibly large feature_027 artifact, and many comparisons have been made. Without validation, I do not accept this as an established association.\n\nH210 tests feature_029 on outcome_014. R201 gives +0.0253 [-0.0358, 0.0864]. The point estimate exceeds the outcome_014 benchmark and the interval contains clinically meaningful positive effects, but it also includes null and is wide. I left it unresolved 