tify claiming a clinically meaningful outcome_016 contrast from this dataset.  \n\nFor effect modification of the accepted feature_065 outcome_013 association, H229/R224 by feature_048 level_001 gives +0.0383, CI -0.0511 to +0.1276: positive and point above benchmark but too imprecise to accept. H228/R223 by feature_019 level_002 is opposite to the registered positive interaction direction and its upper bound is below the outcome_013 benchmark, so I reject that clinically meaningful interaction. H230/R225 by feature_036 level_002 is also opposite to the registered interaction direction; although its upper bound just exceeds benchmark, the estimate and lower bound are strongly negative, and I reject the registered clinically meaningful interaction as unsupported. Overall, these tests do not reveal a clinically meaningful effect modifier of the feature_065 outcome_013 association, which remains a broad association.  \n\nThe outcome_014 screens are mostly subthreshold or inconsistent. H190/R226 feature_063 level_002 versus level_001 gives +0.0361, CI 0.0022 to 0.0701: statistically positive in the registered weaker-dependency direction but lower bound below benchmark, so unresolved. H196/R229 feature_005=1 versus 0 gives +0.0479, CI -0.0006 to +0.0964: point above benchmark but interval includes zero, so unresolved. H191/R227 feature_048 level_001 versus level_003 and H192/R228 feature_011=1 versus 0 are opposite to their registered positive directions and are rejected as unsupported; their wide intervals should not be read as null equivalence, but they do not support the registered hypothesis.  \n\nThe main conclusions remain unchanged: feature_065=1 is associated with stronger outcome_013 dependency; feature_027=1 with outcome_014 is a very large reproducible artifact-like pattern; most other measured predictor-outcome contrasts are null, subthreshold, or unresolved. The feature_006 outcome_014 and feature_065 outcome_016/feature_045 leads remain closed unresolved dataset-internal uncertainties, not accepted causal or generalizable claims.",
